caramos-ota (1.3.6-0caramos1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Release CaramOS 1.3.6.
  * Run all pending timestamp migrations by migration ID ledger.
  * Update Center: the window no longer freezes on a long update log.
    Every output line had its own GLib callback and its own text mark,
    never deleted, so each line cost more than the one before, and the
    window neither redrew nor answered while the callbacks ran. The
    update that finishes an NVIDIA install left unfinished by the 1.3.4
    Driver Check prints 49,000 lines; the window then stopped responding
    for about 16 minutes. Output lines are now shown together at most
    every 100 ms, the progress view keeps the last 2,000 lines, and the
    result page still shows the whole output: 50,000 lines now reach the
    result page in under a second. The window of the installed version
    runs the update, so this takes effect from the update after 1.3.6.
  * Update Center: the progress log shows its last line when many lines
    come at once. GTK scrolled by the height it estimated for the lines
    not laid out yet, and the view stayed at the top of the log; it now
    scrolls once GTK has laid them out.

 -- Vietnam Linux Family <developer@vietnamlinuxfamily.net>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 13:25:41 +0700

caramos-ota (1.3.5-0caramos1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Release CaramOS 1.3.5.
  * Run all pending timestamp migrations by migration ID ledger.
  * Driver Check (tickets H-1110, H-1111): installing a driver broke
    dpkg since 1.3.0. The helper ran apt-get with APT::Status-Fd=1 and
    Dpkg::Use-Pty=0; APT marks its status descriptor close-on-exec, so
    dpkg and every maintainer script ran with stdout closed and failed at
    their first echo. For NVIDIA 580, update-initramfs failed in
    nvidia-kernel-common-580's postinst and every package after it was
    left unpacked: the driver and the kernel it came with did not work,
    while the driver's modprobe file already blacklisted nouveau, so the
    next boot had no graphics driver (1024x768). dpkg keeps the pty APT
    gives it again, as in the App Center. A computer left like this is
    repaired by `sudo dpkg --configure -a`, or by the update to 1.3.5:
    the OTA self-update's apt-get configures the unfinished packages
    (checked on a VM broken by the 1.3.4 helper: exit 0, dpkg clean, the
    NVIDIA modules linked for Linux 7.0).
  * Driver Check: the install log keeps the last state of a line that is
    redrawn, and the objtool warnings from linking the NVIDIA modules
    (49,233 lines for NVIDIA 580) are counted instead of shown.
  * Driver Check checks the free space before installing: APT's download
    and installed sizes, plus an initramfs in /boot for each new kernel
    and a margin, are added up per file system for /, /boot and the APT
    cache. When one falls short nothing is installed and the window says
    how much space is needed and where. NVIDIA 580 on a 1.3.4 install not
    yet updated downloads 940 MiB and takes 2.4 GiB more, with Linux 7.0.
  * Driver Check: the NVIDIA install note says the download can be up to
    about 1 GB, not a few hundred MB.

