caramos-ota (1.3.2-0caramos1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Release CaramOS 1.3.2.
  * Run all pending timestamp migrations by migration ID ledger.
  * New migration 20261018120000_restore_taskbar_after_cinnamon_update
    (ticket H-1046): Mint's Cinnamon 6.6.9 update put back its own panel
    files, so the centre taskbar icons spilled out of their dock. The
    migration makes the CaramOS changes again and sizes the centre zone's
    icons at 32 px. A dpkg trigger on the Cinnamon applet folders runs
    caramos_ota_update.cinnamon_patches after every later Cinnamon update.
  * New migration 20261018120100_update_zalo_native_calls (tickets H-1039,
    H-1042, H-1060): the Zalo that CaramOS or the App Center installed
    moves to Zalo for Linux 26.10.11, which calls without Wine and opens
    quickly. The 170 MB download runs in the background
    (caramos-zalo-update) and the daily check tries again for up to 30
    days, so a slow or missing connection neither fails nor holds up the
    update.
  * Update Center: a failed update offers "Ask for help", which opens
    Feedback & Help with the output and the end of the OTA log attached.
  * Feedback & Help: caramos-help --message TEXT --log FILE starts a bug
    report with logs, as the App Center (0.2.2) and the Update Center do.
    Help requests and bug reports also attach system.log (CaramOS, kernel,
    memory, the warnings and errors of this boot and the end of the
    previous one) and driver-check.log. Logs go in the request's report,
    which only VNLF's moderators see, after e-mail and IP addresses, MAC
    addresses, passwords, the home folder, the computer's name and the
    login name are taken out.
  * Driver Check no longer freezes old computers in the live session. The
    UbuntuDrivers lookup runs only when a one-click driver can come out of
    it (NVIDIA outside the live session, Broadcom Wi-Fi without a driver,
    VirtualBox or VMware), in a separate process at the lowest CPU and disk
    priority with a time and memory limit, and once per computer state.
  * Driver Check reads ACPI devices (GitHub #80): the Intel SST sound
    engine of Atom tablets such as the Lenovo Yoga Book, serial Bluetooth
    and the Atom image processor behind their cameras, and says what is
    missing.

